    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_915dba20ed9a77048a1966325f694b950f93b8d4dfc98fd01e378e706a19381d",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "For X=T^theta with 1<theta<2, pointwise approximation of zeta by a long Dirichlet polynomial does not itself ensure that its weighted mean square agrees with the classical zeta second moment. After the diagonal harmonic sum is evaluated, agreement is reduced to equation (1), an asymptotic identity connecting a compactly supported weight to a floor-weighted integral of its Fourier transform; this unit states that reduction and promises the proof below.",
